### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Each `get_next_item` must be matched by exactly one `item_done`. With `get()`, the item is already completed, so do **not** also call `item_done`.
- `item_done` means the driver is finished with the item. It does not mean the DUT has produced its end-to-end result. The scoreboard tracks that separately.
- The predictor should take inputs from the **monitor** (what was accepted), not from the sequence (what was requested).

### Follow-ups
- *Pipelined driver?* Use `get_next_item` + `item_done` early, then return the response later with `put_response(rsp)` after `rsp.set_id_info(req)`.
- *Why late randomization between start_item and finish_item?* So the item can react to current state (for example a grab or in-line constraints using live values).

answer:
**Answer. **Assume a sampled burst consists of data on every clock where valid is 1, and the first sampled valid=0 ends that burst. Allocate a fresh transaction at the start, accumulate its data, and publish it only at the end. Reset aborts a partial burst in this example. The interface clocking block supplies race-free sampling.

```systemverilog
task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
  packet tr;
  bit collecting = 0;
  forever begin
    @(vif.mon_cb);
    if (vif.mon_cb.rst_n !== 1'b1) begin
      tr = null;
      collecting = 0;
    end else if (vif.mon_cb.valid === 1'b1) begin
      if (!collecting) begin
        tr = packet::type_id::create("tr");
        collecting = 1;
      end
      tr.data.push_back(vif.mon_cb.data);
    end else if (vif.mon_cb.valid === 1'b0) begin
      if (collecting) begin
        ap.write(tr);
        tr = null;     // Do not mutate the published object.
        collecting = 0;
      end
    end else begin
      `uvm_error("VALID_X", "Unknown valid sampled")
    end
  end
endtask
```

packet.data is a queue and ap is a uvm_analysis_port#(packet). A valid/ready protocol needs a different collector: collect only on valid && ready and use its framing rule, such as LAST. A valid falling edge is not a general packet boundary in AXI.

solution:
**Key idea:** Sample on the monitor clocking block. On the first `valid=1`, allocate a **new** transaction. Append data on every cycle with `valid=1`. On the first `valid=0` after that, publish with `ap.write()` and drop the handle, so the subscribers own an object that will not change.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",   "wave": "p.........." },
  { "name": "valid", "wave": "0.1..0.1.0." },
  { "name": "data",  "wave": "x.===x.==x.", "data": ["D0", "D1", "D2", "E0", "E1"] },
  { "name": "event", "wave": "x.=..=.=.=.", "data": ["create", "write pkt1", "create", "write pkt2"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "pkt1 = {D0,D1,D2} published when valid falls; pkt2 = {E0,E1}" } }
```

### Solution
```systemverilog
class packet extends uvm_sequence_item;
  bit [31:0] data[$];
  `uvm_object_utils_begin(packet)
    `uvm_field_queue_int(data, UVM_ALL_ON)
  `uvm_object_utils_end
  function new(string name = "packet"); super.new(name); endfunction
endclass

class burst_monitor extends uvm_monitor;
  `uvm_component_utils(burst_monitor)
  virtual bus_if vif;                       // has clocking block mon_cb
  uvm_analysis_port #(packet) ap;

  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
    ap = new("ap", this);
  endfunction

  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.build_phase(phase);
    if (!uvm_config_db#(virtual bus_if)::get(this, "", "vif", vif))
      `uvm_fatal("NOVIF", "vif not set")
  endfunction

  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    packet tr;
    bit collecting = 0;
    forever begin
      @(vif.mon_cb);
      if (vif.mon_cb.rst_n !== 1'b1) begin          // reset aborts a partial burst
        tr = null; collecting = 0;
      end
      else if (vif.mon_cb.valid === 1'b1) begin
        if (!collecting) begin
          tr = packet::type_id::create("tr");
          collecting = 1;
        end
        tr.data.push_back(vif.mon_cb.data);
      end
      else if (vif.mon_cb.valid === 1'b0) begin
        if (collecting) begin
          `uvm_info("MON", $sformatf("burst of %0d beats", tr.data.size()), UVM_HIGH)
          ap.write(tr);
          tr = null;                                  // never modify after publishing
          collecting = 0;
        end
      end
      else `uvm_error("VALID_X", "valid is X/Z")
    end
  endtask
endclass
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- **Reusing one object** and clearing it after `write()` corrupts every subscriber that stored the handle. Always `create` a new one.
- If valid is still high at end of test, the last burst is never published. Flush or flag it in `extract_phase`/`check_phase`.
- A valid/ready bus needs `valid && ready` per beat and a framing signal such as `last`. Valid going low is **not** a packet boundary in AXI (a master may drop valid between beats).
- X on `valid` should be reported, not silently treated as 0.

### Follow-ups
- *Back-to-back bursts with no gap?* You can't tell them apart without a framing signal (SOP/EOP/last). Raise this ambiguity with the spec owner.
- *Timestamps?* Record `$time` at first and last beat for latency checks in the scoreboard.

answer:
**Answer. **Use a completion mailbox so simultaneous completions are counted without a shared-counter race. Place the worker fork in its own supervisor process. After four completion tokens arrive, disable fork terminates that supervisor's remaining descendants.

```systemverilog
task automatic run_five();
  mailbox #(int) done = new();
  int id;
  fork
    begin : supervisor
      for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) begin
        automatic int k = i;
        fork
          begin
            work(k);       // User-defined automatic task.
            done.put(k);
          end
        join_none
      end
      repeat (4) done.get(id);
      disable fork;
    end
  join
endtask
```

A fifth worker may already have finished in the same time slot; then no worker remains to kill. Killing a process does not automatically release semaphores or repair shared state. Design worker cleanup if cancellation can happen during a resource operation.

solution:
**Key idea:** `uvm_config_db` is a **typed, hierarchy-aware wrapper** around `uvm_resource_db`. It scopes settings with a `(context, inst_path)` pair and has precedence that matches the component tree, so it is the standard choice for component configuration and virtual interfaces. `uvm_resource_db` is the lower-level, globally scoped store, which is useful for things that don't belong to a hierarchy.

| | `uvm_config_db#(T)` | `uvm_resource_db#(T)` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Built on | resource_db | the resource pool |
| Scope | `cntxt.get_full_name() + "." + inst_name` | arbitrary scope string |
| Precedence (build) | Higher in hierarchy wins; same level: last set wins | Explicit precedence value / ordering |
| After build | last set wins | as set |
| Typical use | vif, agent cfg, `is_active` | global shared objects, regex-scoped data |
| API | `set(cntxt, inst, field, val)` / `get(this, "", field, var)` | `set(scope, name, val, accessor)` / `read_by_name(scope, name, val)` |

```systemverilog
// tb_top: per-agent scoped vifs
uvm_config_db#(virtual bus_if)::set(null, "uvm_test_top.env.agt0*", "vif", bus0);
uvm_config_db#(virtual bus_if)::set(null, "uvm_test_top.env.agt1*", "vif", bus1);

// test: test-level setting beats env-level default during build
uvm_config_db#(int)::set(this, "env.agt0.drv", "max_delay", 5);

// driver build_phase
if (!uvm_config_db#(int)::get(this, "", "max_delay", max_delay))
  max_delay = 2;                                     // default

// resource_db: global, not tied to the hierarchy
uvm_resource_db#(int)::set("global", "seed_mode", 1);
void'(uvm_resource_db#(int)::read_by_name("global", "seed_mode", mode));
```

### Explanation
- In build, a `set` made by a component **higher** in the tree wins. The test can override what the env sets for its own children.
- The field type `T` must match exactly between set and get. `int` vs `bit [31:0]` is a miss.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- A wildcard such as `"*"` gives the same vif to every agent, which is a classic multi-agent bug.
- Ignoring `get()`'s return value leaves silent null handles.
- `+UVM_CONFIG_DB_TRACE` prints every set/get, which is the fastest way to debug a missed lookup.

### Follow-ups
- *config_db cost?* Lookups are string/regex matches, so avoid `get` inside per-transaction loops. Get once in build.
- *Config objects vs many fields?* Bundle them into one `agent_cfg` object: one set, one get, type-safe.

