**Answer. **Children created with join_none do not begin executing until the parent suspends or terminates. A child that later reads a shared loop variable can therefore see the final value. Capture the index in an automatic variable in the loop body before the fork.

```verilog
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) begin
  automatic int k = i;
  fork
    $display("k=%0d", k);
  join_none
end
wait fork;
// prints k=0 .. k=4 (order not guaranteed)

// Bug: every child reads the shared i after the loop ends
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
  fork
    $display("i=%0d", i);
  join_none
// prints i=5 five times
```

Putting the capture assignment inside the child is too late, because that child starts after the parent yields. The captured automatic variable belongs to the iteration and remains alive while the spawned process uses it.

