solution:
**Key idea:** In a serialized driver each acquired request is retired exactly once, *after* the bus completes, and a single thread owns the pins, including during reset. Polling with `try_next_item` every clock lets that one thread hold the pins idle while reset is asserted.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",        "wave": "p.........." },
  { "name": "rst_n",      "wave": "0.1........" },
  { "name": "pins",       "wave": "=..=...=..=", "data": ["idle", "drive req0", "idle", "req1"] },
  { "name": "done",       "wave": "0......10.." },
  { "name": "try_next",   "wave": "0..1......1" },
  { "name": "item_done",  "wave": "0.......10." }
],
  "head": { "text": "poll every clock; idle in reset; item_done(rsp) once after completion" } }
```

### Explanation
Interview answer: "My driver polls with `try_next_item` on every clock, so the same thread that owns the pins can also hold them idle during reset. It copies the routing ID into the response and calls `item_done` once, after the bus completes."

### Code
```systemverilog
class bus_driver extends uvm_driver #(bus_req, bus_rsp);
  `uvm_component_utils(bus_driver)
  virtual bus_if vif;

  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
  endfunction

  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    bus_req req;
    bus_rsp rsp;
    drive_status_e status;
    forever begin
      @(vif.cb);
      if (vif.cb.rst_n !== 1'b1) begin   // !== also catches X/Z on reset
        drive_idle();
        continue;
      end
      seq_item_port.try_next_item(req); // non-blocking: null if nothing queued
      if (req == null) begin
        drive_idle();
        continue;
      end
      rsp = bus_rsp::type_id::create("rsp");
      rsp.set_id_info(req);             // route response to the right sequence
      drive_one(req, rsp, status);      // blocks until bus completion or reset abort
      rsp.status = status;
      drive_idle();
      seq_item_port.item_done(rsp);     // exactly once per acquired request
    end
  endtask
  // drive_one() and drive_idle() are protocol specific (see the APB template)
endclass
```

**Shorter blocking form:** `get_next_item(req); drive_one(req, rsp, status); item_done(rsp);` is fine only if something else keeps the pins idle during reset while the driver is blocked waiting for an item.

**APB outline:** drive setup (`psel=1, penable=0`), next cycle access (`penable=1`), hold address/control until `pready`, sample `prdata`/`pslverr`, then go idle or start the next setup.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Reset in the middle of `drive_one`: abort, set `status = RESET`, and still call `item_done(rsp)` once so the sequence is not stuck.
- Using `rst_n == 0` instead of `!== 1'b1` lets an X reset be treated as "out of reset".
- A second process that clears pins on reset creates multiple drivers on the same signal; keep one owner.

### Follow-ups
- *Sync or async reset?* Ask. With a clocking block, reset is seen on the next sampled edge; async pin clearing needs a redesigned ownership model.
- *Does an in-flight item abort or retry?* It is a spec decision; the sequence sees `status == RESET` and decides.
- *How does the scoreboard flush cancelled predictions?* The monitor publishes a reset event in the same analysis stream.
- *Change the driver for one test only?* Factory type override of a derived driver from that test's `build_phase`.

