solution:
**Key idea:** The solver satisfies all constraints at once, so list domain, size, pairwise, and sum rules before writing any. Write size first, then `foreach` domains, then uniqueness, then sums cast to a wide-enough type, and always check `randomize()`'s return value.

Worked example (one legal solution of the first block):

| index | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | sum |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `a[i]` | 3 | 12 | 0 | 9 | 15 | 7 | 6 | 8 | 60 |

With `bit [3:0]` elements and no cast, `a.sum()` is 4 bits wide and wraps at 16, so the solver could satisfy "sum == 12" with a real sum of 60.

### Code
```systemverilog
class arr_item;
  rand int unsigned a[];
  constraint c {
    a.size() == 8;
    foreach (a[i]) a[i] inside {[0:15]};
    unique {a};
    // without unique: foreach (a[i]) foreach (a[j]) if (i < j) a[i] != a[j];
    a.sum() with (int'(item)) == 60;   // max sum 8*15 = 120 fits int
  }
endclass

// Exactly N ones without $countones (bit array)
//   bits.sum() with (int'(item)) == N;

// Each of 6 values exactly 40 times, no two consecutive zeros
class cnt_item;
  rand bit [2:0] arr[];
  int vals[6] = '{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
  constraint each_c {
    arr.size() == 240;                                   // 6 values x 40
    foreach (vals[k]) arr.sum() with (int'(item == vals[k])) == 40;
    foreach (arr[i]) if (i > 0) !(arr[i] == 0 && arr[i-1] == 0);
  }
endclass

// Three ways to override a parent constraint
class base_item extends uvm_sequence_item;
  `uvm_object_utils(base_item)
  rand bit [7:0] len;
  constraint len_c  { len inside {[1:16]}; }
  constraint dflt_c { soft len == 4; }
  function new(string name = "base_item"); super.new(name); endfunction
endclass

class long_item extends base_item;           // 1) same name replaces parent block
  `uvm_object_utils(long_item)
  constraint len_c { len inside {[64:128]}; }
  function new(string name = "long_item"); super.new(name); endfunction
endclass

// 2) at the call site
//   item.len_c.constraint_mode(0);
//   if (!item.randomize() with { len == 200; }) `uvm_fatal("RAND", "failed")
// 3) soft default gives way to a hard constraint
//   if (!item.randomize() with { len == 8; }) `uvm_fatal("RAND", "failed")
// 4) from the test, no class/sequence edits
//   base_item::type_id::set_type_override(long_item::get_type());
```

### Rules to quote
| Control | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| same-name constraint in child | replaces parent block |
| `constraint_mode(0)` | disables a block for this object |
| `soft` | default that any conflicting hard constraint overrides |
| `rand_mode(0)` | freezes one variable |
| `pre_/post_randomize` | run around the solver |
| `local::name` in `with` | refers to the caller's variable, not the item's |

Item constraints describe legality everywhere; inline sequence constraints describe one scenario.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- More unique values than the domain allows (9 unique values in `[0:7]`) fails.
- Contradictory sums, or a narrow sum that silently overflows.
- A child constraint that conflicts with a parent block you forgot existed.
- The 300-element version of the count problem needs a domain for the other 60 elements; clarify the spec.

