- `std::randomize()` returns 0 on failure and leaves variables unchanged; ignoring the return value silently reuses stale values.
- `shuffle()` runs after the solver and can break adjacency rules: express order in constraints or re-check after shuffling.
- History lives in the object: creating a new object per transaction resets it; keep history in the sequence or a static/shared variable if needed.

