### Explanation
Interview answer: "I timestamp edges with `$realtime` and compare the difference with the bound. Same-direction edges give the period, opposite edges give high or low time. I discard the first measurement after reset because it starts from an arbitrary point."

### Code
```systemverilog
module clk_checker #(realtime EXP = 10ns, realtime MIN_HIGH = 2.5ns) (input logic sig);
  timeunit 1ns; timeprecision 1ps;
  realtime t_rise;
  bit      have_rise;

  always @(posedge sig) begin
    realtime p;
    p = $realtime - t_rise;
    if (have_rise && (p < EXP*0.95 || p > EXP*1.05))     // 5% tolerance
      $error("period %0t outside +/-5%% of %0t", p, EXP);
    t_rise    = $realtime;
    have_rise = 1;
  end

  always @(negedge sig)
    if (have_rise && ($realtime - t_rise) < MIN_HIGH)
      $error("high pulse shorter than %0t", MIN_HIGH);

  // Assertion form: local variable carries the rise time
  property p_min_high(realtime min_w);
    realtime t;
    @(posedge sig) (1, t = $realtime) |=> @(negedge sig) ($realtime - t) >= min_w;
  endproperty
  ap_min_high: assert property (p_min_high(MIN_HIGH));
endmodule

// Testbench clock with random start level, random phase, and jitter
module jitter_clk #(int HALF_PS = 5000, int JIT_PS = 100) (output logic clk);
  timeunit 1ns; timeprecision 1ps;
  initial begin
    clk = $urandom_range(0, 1);
    #($urandom_range(0, 1000) * 1ps);
    forever begin
      int j;
      j = int'($urandom_range(0, 2*JIT_PS)) - JIT_PS;   // signed jitter
      #((HALF_PS + j) * 1ps) clk = ~clk;
    end
  end
endmodule
```

### Rules to quote
- Keep parentheses around the two bound checks: `&&` binds tighter than `||`, so `have_rise && p < lo || p > hi` reports a false error on the first edge.
- Use `|=>` in the multi-clock property: antecedent and consequent use different clocks.
- Delay of 0 to 1.25 periods: `#($urandom_range(0, 125) * PERIOD / 100)` with `timeprecision` fine enough for the step.
- If the expected period changes asynchronously, latch it at each measured edge and define which interval uses the old value.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- A pulse shorter than the timescale precision rounds to zero.
- Rounding exactly at the bound; use a small epsilon or integer picoseconds.
- `$time` returns an integer in the module's time unit and truncates; use `$realtime`.

