|---|---|---|---|
| READ | 1000_3A57 | 111 | yes (any) |
| WRITE | 1000_9C40 | 000 | yes (and natural for size 8) |
| ATOMIC | 1000_0128 | 000 | yes |

### Explanation
- `if/else` and `->` are bidirectional for the solver: it may pick an address first and then a compatible command — use `solve cmd before addr;` if you want the command distribution unaffected by address choice.
- Both `c_align` and `c_nat_align` apply together; a WRITE of size 8 must satisfy both → 8-byte aligned.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- `addr % ALIGN` with `ALIGN == 0` is undefined/fails; with signed `int` types, `%` can be negative.
- The range must contain at least one aligned address; `[0x1001 : 0x1003]` makes WRITE infeasible.
- Always check `randomize()` return.

