Interface signals can be accessed via a virtual interface handle that points to the actual physical interface. Signals within the DUT can be accessed directly by providing a hierarchical RTL path to `uvm_hdl_*` functions such as.

```systemverilog
uvm_hdl_force("top.eatable.fruits.apple.slice", 2);
uvm_hdl_deposit("top.eatable.fruits.apple.slice", 3);
uvm_hdl_read("top.eatable.fruits.apple.slice", rdata);
```

solution:
**Key idea:** Interface signals are reached through a **virtual interface** handle that the top module puts in `uvm_config_db`. Internal DUT signals (not on any interface) can be reached by hierarchical path with the DPI-based `uvm_hdl_*` functions: `uvm_hdl_read`, `uvm_hdl_deposit`, `uvm_hdl_force`, `uvm_hdl_release`, `uvm_hdl_check_path`.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  TOP["tb_top<br/>dut_if vif(clk)"] -->|"config_db set vif"| CDB["uvm_config_db"]
  CDB -->|"get in build_phase"| DRV["driver / monitor<br/>vif.cb.data"]
  SEQ["sequence"] -->|"p_sequencer.vif or cfg.vif"| CDB
  SEQ -->|"uvm_hdl_read path"| DUT["DUT internal reg<br/>top.dut.u_core.state"]
```

### Code
```systemverilog
// tb_top
module tb_top;
  logic clk = 0;
  always #5 clk = ~clk;
  dut_if vif(clk);
  dut u_dut(.clk(clk), .data(vif.data), .valid(vif.valid));
  initial begin
    uvm_config_db#(virtual dut_if)::set(null, "uvm_test_top.*", "vif", vif);
    run_test();
  end
endmodule

// In a component
class my_monitor extends uvm_monitor;
  `uvm_component_utils(my_monitor)
  virtual dut_if vif;
  function new(string name, uvm_component parent); super.new(name, parent); endfunction
  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.build_phase(phase);
    if (!uvm_config_db#(virtual dut_if)::get(this, "", "vif", vif))
      `uvm_fatal("NOVIF", "virtual interface not set")
  endfunction
  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    forever @(posedge vif.clk) if (vif.valid) `uvm_info("MON", $sformatf("data=%h", vif.data), UVM_HIGH)
  endtask
endclass

// In a sequence (sequences are objects: get the vif via config_db or the sequencer)
task body();
  uvm_hdl_data_t v;
  if (!uvm_hdl_check_path("tb_top.u_dut.u_core.state"))
    `uvm_fatal("PATH", "bad HDL path")
  void'(uvm_hdl_read   ("tb_top.u_dut.u_core.state", v));
  void'(uvm_hdl_deposit("tb_top.u_dut.u_core.cnt", 3));   // value until next driver update
  void'(uvm_hdl_force  ("tb_top.u_dut.u_core.err", 1));   // held until release
  void'(uvm_hdl_release("tb_top.u_dut.u_core.err"));
endtask
```

| Method | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| virtual interface | normal, portable access to interface pins (with clocking blocks) |
| `uvm_hdl_read` | read any signal by string path |
| `uvm_hdl_deposit` | set once; logic may overwrite next cycle |
| `uvm_hdl_force` / `release` | override until released |

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- `uvm_hdl_*` needs DPI and read/write access to the path (e.g. `+acc`/`-access +rw` flags); they return 0 on failure, so check the result.
- Hard-coded paths break when the hierarchy changes; keep them in one config object.
- A sequence has no `build_phase`: use `uvm_config_db::get(null, get_full_name(), ...)` or `p_sequencer.vif`.

### Follow-ups
- *Why not use `$root.tb_top.dut.sig` directly in a class?* Hierarchical references inside packages are not allowed; virtual interfaces and DPI paths keep classes in packages.

