### Explanation
- Synthesis tools can convert `if (en) q <= d` for a bank of flops into an ICG automatically (clock-gating insertion).
- Clock enable: simpler timing and verification; gating: lower power, but adds clock skew and requires CTS handling.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Never gate a clock with a plain AND/OR gate driven by combinational logic: glitches create extra edges.

### Follow-ups
- *What is `SE` on an ICG?* Scan enable forces the clock on during test.

**Key idea:** A dual-port RAM is written in the write domain and read in the read domain. Each side keeps a binary pointer plus a Gray-coded copy; the Gray pointer is synchronized into the other domain with 2 flops and compared there to produce `full` (write side) and `empty` (read side).

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  subgraph WD["write clock domain"]
    WP["wptr bin/gray"] --> FULL["full logic"]
  end
  subgraph RD["read clock domain"]
    RP["rptr bin/gray"] --> EMPTY["empty logic"]
  end
  WP -->|"waddr"| MEM["dual-port RAM"]
  RP -->|"raddr"| MEM
  WP -->|"wgray 2FF sync"| EMPTY
  RP -->|"rgray 2FF sync"| FULL
```

```systemverilog
module async_fifo #(parameter int DW = 8, AW = 4) (
  input  logic wclk, wrst_n, winc, input logic [DW-1:0] wdata, output logic wfull,
  input  logic rclk, rrst_n, rinc, output logic [DW-1:0] rdata, output logic rempty);
  logic [DW-1:0] mem [2**AW];
  logic [AW:0] wbin, wgray, rbin, rgray, wq1_rgray, wq2_rgray, rq1_wgray, rq2_wgray;
  logic [AW:0] wbin_n, wgray_n, rbin_n, rgray_n;

  // write side
  assign wbin_n  = wbin + (winc & ~wfull);
  assign wgray_n = (wbin_n >> 1) ^ wbin_n;
  always_ff @(posedge wclk or negedge wrst_n)
    if (!wrst_n) {wbin, wgray, wfull} <= '0;
    else begin
      wbin  <= wbin_n;  wgray <= wgray_n;
      wfull <= (wgray_n == {~wq2_rgray[AW:AW-1], wq2_rgray[AW-2:0]});
    end
  always_ff @(posedge wclk) if (winc && !wfull) mem[wbin[AW-1:0]] <= wdata;
  always_ff @(posedge wclk or negedge wrst_n)
    if (!wrst_n) {wq2_rgray, wq1_rgray} <= '0; else {wq2_rgray, wq1_rgray} <= {wq1_rgray, rgray};

  // read side
  assign rbin_n  = rbin + (rinc & ~rempty);
  assign rgray_n = (rbin_n >> 1) ^ rbin_n;
  always_ff @(posedge rclk or negedge rrst_n)
    if (!rrst_n) begin {rbin, rgray} <= '0; rempty <= 1'b1; end
    else begin
      rbin <= rbin_n; rgray <= rgray_n;
      rempty <= (rgray_n == rq2_wgray);
    end
  assign rdata = mem[rbin[AW-1:0]];
  always_ff @(posedge rclk or negedge rrst_n)
    if (!rrst_n) {rq2_wgray, rq1_wgray} <= '0; else {rq2_wgray, rq1_wgray} <= {rq1_wgray, wgray};
endmodule
```

### Explanation
- Pointers are AW+1 bits: the extra MSB distinguishes full (wrapped once more) from empty (equal).
- Flags are *pessimistic*: full is seen late deasserting, empty is seen late deasserting, never falsely clear -> safe.
- Based on Cummings, "Simulation and Synthesis Techniques for Asynchronous FIFO Design".

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Depth must be a power of two for this Gray scheme.
- Gray pointers must be registered (not combinational) before crossing.

### Follow-ups
- *Why is pessimism OK?* It only costs throughput, never correctness.

**Key idea:** `a |-> b` (overlapped): if `a` matches, `b` must hold in the **same** clock tick where `a` ended. `a |=> b` (non-overlapped): `b` must hold on the **next** tick; it is equivalent to `a |-> ##1 b`.

```systemverilog
// grant in the same cycle as request
p_same: assert property (@(posedge clk) req |-> gnt);
// grant one cycle after request
p_next: assert property (@(posedge clk) req |=> gnt);
// equivalent
p_next2: assert property (@(posedge clk) req |-> ##1 gnt);
```

```wavedrom
{"signal": [{"name": "clk", "wave": "p......"}, {"name": "req", "wave": "0.10..."}, {"name": "gnt (|-> passes)", "wave": "0.10..."}, {"name": "gnt (|=> passes)", "wave": "0..10.."}], "head": {"text": "req sampled high at edge 2: |-> checks gnt at edge 2, |=> at edge 3"}}
```

### Explanation
- If the antecedent is false the property passes vacuously (watch for vacuity in coverage).
- Both are only allowed in properties, not in sequences.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- A common off-by-one bug is writing `|->` when the design responds one cycle later.

### Follow-ups
- *How to check vacuity?* Add `cover property (req)` or use tool vacuity reports.

**Key idea:** `$past(sig, N)` returns the sampled value N clock ticks ago (default 1); `$rose(sig)` is true when the LSB sampled value went 0->1 since the previous tick; `$fell(sig)` when it went 1->0. `$stable` and `$changed` complete the set.

```systemverilog
// data must be stable while valid && !ready (backpressure)
a_hold: assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
  $past(valid && !ready) |-> $stable(data) && valid);

// a rising start must be followed by busy next cycle
a_busy: assert property (@(posedge clk) $rose(start) |=> busy);

// counter increments by 1 when en
a_inc: assert property (@(posedge clk) en |=> cnt == $past(cnt) + 1'b1);
```

```wavedrom
{"signal": [{"name": "clk", "wave": "p......"}, {"name": "start", "wave": "0.1..0."}, {"name": "$rose(start)", "wave": "0.10..."}, {"name": "$fell(start)", "wave": "0....10"}], "head": {"text": "sampled-value functions compare the current and previous sampled value"}}
```

### Explanation
- Values are from the Preponed region, so `$rose` is true at the first clock edge where the sampled value is 1 and the previous was 0.
- `$past(x, 2, en)` uses a gating expression: only ticks where `en` was true count.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- At the first clock tick, `$past` returns the initial value (often X/0) -> guard with reset or `disable iff`.

### Follow-ups
- *`$rose` on a vector?* Only the LSB is considered; use `$changed` for vectors.

**Key idea:** A skid buffer registers `ready` toward the upstream (breaking the combinational ready path) and adds one spare register to catch the item that was sent in the cycle when downstream ready dropped. It keeps full throughput with registered ready.

```systemverilog
module skid_buffer #(parameter int W = 32) (
  input  logic clk, rst_n,
  input  logic         s_valid, output logic s_ready, input  logic [W-1:0] s_data,
  output logic         m_valid, input  logic m_ready, output logic [W-1:0] m_data);
  logic         skid_valid;
  logic [W-1:0] skid_data;

  // upstream ready is a register: high when the skid slot is empty
  always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge rst_n)
    if (!rst_n) begin
      skid_valid <= 1'b0; m_valid <= 1'b0;
    end else begin
      if (s_ready) begin                      // can accept from upstream
        if (m_valid && !m_ready) begin        // output stalled -> park in skid
          skid_valid <= s_valid;
          skid_data  <= s_data;
        end else begin                        // output free -> pass through
          m_valid <= s_valid;
          m_data  <= s_data;
        end
      end else if (m_ready) begin             // drain skid into output
        m_valid    <= 1'b1;
        m_data     <= skid_data;
        skid_valid <= 1'b0;
      end
    end
  assign s_ready = !skid_valid;
endmodule
```

```wavedrom
{"signal": [{"name": "clk", "wave": "p......."}, {"name": "s_valid", "wave": "01......"}, {"name": "s_data", "wave": "x====.==", "data": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E", "F"]}, {"name": "m_ready", "wave": "1..01..."}, {"name": "s_ready", "wave": "1...01.."}, {"name": "skid", "wave": "x...=x..", "data": ["C"]}, {"name": "m_data", "wave": "x.==.===", "data": ["A", "B", "B", "C", "D"]}], "head": {"text": "m_ready drops: C goes into the skid slot, s_ready drops a cycle later, nothing is lost"}}
```

### Explanation
- Two storage slots (output register + skid register); `s_ready` depends only on a flop -> no combinational path from m_ready to s_ready.
- Throughput stays one item per cycle when m_ready is high.
- Also called a register slice (AXI) or elastic buffer.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Only the valid flags need reset; data registers can stay un-reset. Verify with randomized m_ready (including long stalls) and an in-order scoreboard.

### Follow-ups
- *Why not just register ready?* Without the skid slot, the item sent during the late-ready cycle would be lost.

**Key idea:** An interface bundles related signals (plus modports, clocking blocks, tasks and assertions) and can be parameterized for widths/options, giving a reusable connection for both RTL and testbench.

```systemverilog
interface axis_if #(parameter int DW = 32, parameter bit HAS_LAST = 1) (input logic clk, rst_n);
  logic          tvalid, tready;
  logic [DW-1:0] tdata;
  logic          tlast;

  modport src (output tvalid, tdata, tlast, input tready, clk, rst_n);
  modport snk (input  tvalid, tdata, tlast, output tready, input clk, rst_n);

  clocking drv_cb @(posedge clk);
    default input #1step output #1;
    output tvalid, tdata, tlast; input tready;
  endclocking

  a_hold: assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n)
            tvalid && !tready |=> tvalid && $stable(tdata));
endinterface

axis_if #(.DW(64)) s_axis (.clk(clk), .rst_n(rst_n));
```

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  SRC["producer (modport src)"] --> IF["axis_if #(DW=64)"] --> SNK["consumer (modport snk)"]
  TB["UVM driver (virtual axis_if)"] -.-> IF
```

### Explanation
- Parameters must match between the interface instance and the virtual interface type in the TB (`virtual axis_if #(64)`).
- Embedding protocol assertions in the interface checks every instance automatically.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Parameterized virtual interfaces make UVM components parameterized too; a common workaround is using max width or an abstract class (polymorphic interface).

### Follow-ups
- *Can interfaces be synthesized?* Yes, flattened to signals by synthesis.

**Key idea:** A modport defines a directional view of an interface for a particular user (master, slave, monitor), so the compiler can check that a module only drives what it should.

```systemverilog
interface bus_if (input logic clk);
  logic req, gnt;
  logic [31:0] addr;
  modport master  (input clk, gnt, output req, addr);
  modport slave   (input clk, req, addr, output gnt);
  modport monitor (input clk, req, gnt, addr);
endinterface

module cpu (bus_if.master bus);
  always_ff @(posedge bus.clk) bus.req <= 1'b1;   // legal
  // assign bus.gnt = 1'b1;                       // compile error: gnt is input
endmodule
```

| Modport | req | gnt | addr |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| master | output | input | output |
| slave | input | output | input |
| monitor | input | input | input |

### Explanation
- Modports can also export/import tasks and functions and include clocking blocks (`modport tb (clocking cb)`).
- Selecting the modport can be done in the module header or at instantiation (`cpu u_cpu(.bus(b.master))`).

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Without a modport, all signals are inout-like and direction errors go undetected.

### Follow-ups
- *Virtual interface with modport?* `virtual bus_if.master vif;` restricts the class view as well.

**Key idea:** A virtual interface is a variable in a class that holds a handle to a real (static) interface instance, letting dynamic class-based TB components (drivers, monitors) access DUT signals without hard-coded hierarchical paths. In UVM it is passed through `uvm_config_db`.

```systemverilog
// top module
module tb_top;
  bit clk;
  axis_if vif_inst (.clk(clk), .rst_n(rst_n));
  dut u_dut (.s_axis(vif_inst));
  initial begin
    uvm_config_db#(virtual axis_if)::set(null, "uvm_test_top.env.agt*", "vif", vif_inst);
    run_test();
  end
endmodule

// driver
class axis_driver extends uvm_driver #(axis_item);
  virtual axis_if vif;
  function void build_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    super.build_phase(phase);
    if (!uvm_config_db#(virtual axis_if)::get(this, "", "vif", vif))
      `uvm_fatal("NOVIF", "virtual interface not set")
  endfunction
endclass
```

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  TOP["tb_top: axis_if instance"] -->|config_db set| CDB["uvm_config_db"]
  CDB -->|get in build_phase| DRV["driver.vif"]
  CDB -->|get| MON["monitor.vif"]
  DRV --> PINS["DUT pins via vif"]
```

### Explanation
- Classes cannot contain static interface instances; virtual interfaces bridge static and dynamic worlds.
- The same component can be reused for multiple instances by pointing it to different interfaces.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Null virtual interface at run time -> fatal; always check the `get` return value.

### Follow-ups
- *Abstract class alternative?* 'Polymorphic interface': an abstract API class implemented inside the interface.

**Key idea:** A clocking block groups signals synchronous to a clock and defines when the TB samples them (input skew, default #1step = just before the edge) and when it drives them (output skew after the edge). The TB thus never reads or writes at the exact same instant the DUT does.

```systemverilog
interface bus_if (input logic clk);
  logic req, gnt;
  clocking cb @(posedge clk);
    default input #1step output #1ns;
    output req;
    input  gnt;
  endclocking
  modport tb (clocking cb);
endinterface

// in driver
@(vif.cb);                 // wait for clocking event
vif.cb.req <= 1'b1;        // driven 1ns after the edge
if (vif.cb.gnt) ...        // value sampled 1step before the edge
```

```wavedrom
{"signal": [{"name": "clk", "wave": "0.1...0.1..."}, {"name": "gnt (DUT)", "wave": "0..1........"}, {"name": "cb.gnt sampled", "wave": "0.......1..."}, {"name": "cb.req driven", "wave": "0..1........"}], "head": {"text": "input sampled just before the edge (#1step); output driven after it (#1ns)"}}
```

### Explanation
- Clocking-block drives are applied in the Re-NBA region; sampling uses Preponed values, so DUT and TB orders don't matter.
- `##N` delays count clocking events of the default clocking block.

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Mixing direct signal writes (`vif.req = 1`) with clocking-block drives on the same signal causes confusing behavior.

### Follow-ups
- *Input skew #0?* Samples in Observed region (after design settles) - can see same-edge DUT updates.

**Key idea:** interface 解決「連線」問題；clocking block 解決「時序競爭（race）」問題：輸入在時鐘前 skew 取樣，輸出在時鐘後 skew 驅動。

