//   seq.ctrl_value = 32'h0000_0001;
//   phase.raise_objection(this);
//   seq.start(env.agt.seqr);
//   phase.drop_objection(this);
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Checking `polls >= 100` without `!status[0]` reports a timeout even when the 100th read succeeded.
- If the driver uses a separate response (`put_response`), call `get_response(rsp)` and read `rsp.data` instead of `req.data`.
- With a RAL model the same flow is `regmodel.CTRL.write()`, a `STATUS.read()` loop (or `mirror`), then a datapath write.

### Follow-ups
- **What if `randomize()` fails?** Stop with `uvm_fatal`; never `finish_item` an unrandomized item.
- **Why not `@(posedge clk)` in the sequence?** A sequence usually has no vif, and clock counting doesn't line up with bus timing: just issue another read.
- **Who ends the sim after a timeout?** The test, via its objection.

**Key idea:** two instances of the *same* config sequence (different `base_addr`) run on two sequencers inside `fork ... join`; the datapath sequence is the next statement after `join`, so it can't start until the slower block finishes.

```wavedrom
{ "signal": [
  { "name": "clk",             "wave": "p............." },
  { "name": "bus A (seqr_a)",  "wave": "x=====xxxxxxxx", "data": ["A0", "A1", "A2", "A3", "A4"] },
  { "name": "bus B (seqr_b)",  "wave": "x=.=.=.=.=.xxx", "data": ["B0", "B1", "B2", "B3", "B4"] },
  { "name": "datapath (join)", "wave": "xxxxxxxxxxx===", "data": ["D0", "D1", "D2"] },
  {},
  { "name": "datapath if join_any (BUG)", "wave": "xxxxxx===xxxxx", "data": ["D0", "D1", "D2"] }
],
  "head": { "text": "join waits for the slower block B; join_any starts traffic while B is still configuring" } }
```

| fork variant | returns when | effect here |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `join` | all branches done | correct |
| `join_any` | first branch done | datapath races B's config |
| `join_none` | immediately | datapath starts before any config |

### Code
```systemverilog
class block_config_seq extends uvm_sequence #(reg_seq_item);
  `uvm_object_utils(block_config_seq)

  bit [31:0] base_addr;

  function new(string name = "block_config_seq"); super.new(name); endfunction

  task body();
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) begin
      reg_seq_item req = reg_seq_item::type_id::create($sformatf("req%0d", i));
      start_item(req);
      if (!req.randomize() with {
            addr       == base_addr + i*4;
            data       == 32'h1000 + i;
            read_write == 0;
          })
        `uvm_fatal(get_full_name(), "randomize failed")
      finish_item(req);
    end
  endtask
endclass

class top_vseq extends uvm_sequence;
  `uvm_object_utils(top_vseq)
  `uvm_declare_p_sequencer(virtual_sequencer)

  function new(string name = "top_vseq"); super.new(name); endfunction

  task body();
    block_config_seq a  = block_config_seq::type_id::create("a");
    block_config_seq b  = block_config_seq::type_id::create("b");
    datapath_seq     dp = datapath_seq::type_id::create("dp");

    a.base_addr = 32'h1000;
    b.base_addr = 32'h2000;

    fork
      a.start(p_sequencer.seqr_a);
      b.start(p_sequencer.seqr_b);
    join                               // both configs complete

    dp.start(p_sequencer.seqr_dp);
  endtask
endclass
```

### Corner cases & pitfalls
- Both handles pointing to the same sequencer: the waveform shows ten serialized writes, not two parallel streams.
- One sequence object can't be started twice concurrently: create `a` and `b` separately.
- Variable `fork` inside a loop: wrap each branch's loop variable in an `automatic` copy, or all branches see the final value.

### Follow-ups
- **What exactly is started inside the `fork`?** Sequence objects, each on its own sequencer.
- **What goes wrong with `join_any`?** The slower block may still be writing registers while traffic starts.
- **Need A to finish first, then B and traffic in parallel?** Nest: `a.start(...); fork b.start(...); dp.start(...); join`.
